WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.691
From route to row and back: one request, five floors, and the shape of the answer on each.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.068
A request arrives: a GET to /api/tasks/7.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:20.186
The route file only matches. Path and method in, a controller out. No logic here.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:29.415
The controller speaks HTTP. It reads the id from the request, and turns the text "7" into a number.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.208
Then it asks the model for the task, and waits.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.807
It never writes SQL.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.066
The model is the only floor that knows there is a database.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:47.350
One method per operation. Here: findById, with the number 7.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.965
It never touches the request or the response.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.849
Below the model, the pool. Created once, at start-up, from the DATABASE_URL in the environment.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:06.626
It keeps a few connections open, and lends one to each query. No new login per request.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:13.458
Create it inside a handler, and connections pile up until the database says: too many clients.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.252
The query leaves in two parts. The SQL text, with a placeholder: $1.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.910
And the values: an array holding 7, travelling apart.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:32.742
The database compiles the text first. Only then does the 7 drop into the slot.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.220
Why apart? Suppose the input is a trick string that is always true.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.925
Glued into the text, it becomes SQL, and the query matches every row.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.502
Sent as a value, it is just an odd string that matches nothing.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:56.146
A template literal is still gluing. Only a placeholder keeps input out of the SQL.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:03.482
One request, several changes that belong together? Take one client from the pool.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.263
Each pool query may use a different connection, so BEGIN and COMMIT would never meet.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:15.906
Use that one client from BEGIN to COMMIT. ROLLBACK on an error, and always give it back.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.155
Now the answer climbs back. The driver hands the model an array of rows.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.290
The model takes the first one: id 7, Draft project plan.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.851
But a row is not the answer yet. The model maps it, in one place.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:42.384
snake_case column names become camelCase keys. user_id turns into userId.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:50.750
Dates become ISO strings. And only the fields you list leave the model, so a password hash never leaks by accident.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:59.127
Now the controller holds a plain object. It picks the status, 200, and sends JSON.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.355
Ask for task 999 instead. No row comes back.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:11.804
The model does not fail. A missing row is a normal answer: null.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:18.263
The controller turns null into a 404. Only the controller speaks HTTP.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.843
Because the model is handed its database, a test can hand it a fake. No server needed.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:31.937
And the same method names work over MongoDB. The controller never changes.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.689
Practise it with the SQL runner in Relational databases.
